[Music]  [Music]  when you need to see the bigger picture  sometimes it's best to return to your  roots Assassin's Creed origins takes us  thinking to heart and steps into the  seamless and dynamic open-world of  ancient Egypt although this move shows  glimmers of a brighter future for the  series it also becomes clear that it's  core gameplay and presentation have some  difficulties keeping up with the  newfound pace and scale learn the lines  between prequel and sequel Assassin's  Creed origins takes us back to the  beginning of the assassin Brotherhood  while also laying the groundwork for a  new present-day storyline set during the  time of the Ptolemaic Kingdom in Egypt  you take on the role of byuk a Magi  ranger who embarks on a quest for  revenge against a mysterious order that  predates the templars he'll sneak loot  and stab key figures in the social and  political worlds of Egypt leading to  unforeseen consequences for the future  of the kingdom right up to the present  day events they told me from my throne  and why should I get above them the  snake has many heads those heads make up  the order in its 30 our main story  you'll travel to a number of areas  across Egypt meeting different allies  and villains with their own agendas  while references to significant  characters and events from other games  are present they take a backseat to the  twin narratives of origins particularly  - that a bike and his companions as they  lay the foundations of their Creed in  Egypt moving steadily away from the  somewhat identical formula of past  Assassin's Creed games where you  gradually move out to different hub  areas and tackled largely isolated  missions origins gives you a greater  level of freedom and agency in a more  seamless world where you take part in  activities at your leisure with each  region possessing several points of  interest veering off the paths to find  sites unseen can yield valuable loot  history about the world and other  secrets that tie into something greater  while some locations share a few too  many similarities in style and general  landscape and others are frustratingly  sparse with content and activity the  world is beautifully realized with great  attention to detail  which houses a number of AI systems that  play off one another from the flocks of  birds that rise in unison while running  through marshlands to the crocodiles  that pluck sailors off their boats  trekking through the many rivers the  aesthetic and wool design of Assassin's  Creed origins is one of the series's  finest achievements it thankfully makes  good on the potential of its setting  showing off the clash of ideologies and  cultures during Ptolemaic Egypt in  giving it life in a number of surprising  ways illustrating the conflict between  greco-roman and Egyptian influences the  core narrative and sight stories are  engaging and feel meaningful even  tackling issues of racism colonialism  and the systemic misogyny at the times  this is especially relevant to biock who  is himself an outsider in many of the  locations he travels to and as a result  witnesses some of the abuse firsthand  origins definitely handles its a world  tone by balancing moments of heartbreak  and earnestness such as side stories  dealing with greedy landowners poisoning  lower-class citizens with moments of  levity like when bike entertains  children across the many cities in Egypt  unfortunately several technical hiccups  and bugs crop up which can really  disrupt the flow of the experience  during our playthrough of the Xbox one X  and ps4 versions instances of texture  pop-in  noticeable framerate dips during  cutscenes and gameplay and odd graphical  issues impact moments and events that  should be meaningful these issues  unfortunately persist throughout  slightly dragging down the otherwise  incredible setting  [Music]  origins also make some overhauls of the  style and tone of combat trading steel  with enemies now feels more active and  involved thanks to its dynamic strikes  and real-time blocking and parrying  while certain traces of the series is  group-oriented action remain combat  focuses more on the smaller skirmishes  where you pick your moments and strike  at the right time  moreover range combat has also been  improved adding greater maneuverability  and accuracy including a useful slow-mo  aim while leaping through the air though  this new approach to combat it initially  feels like an improvement all around  making battles more engaging and  involved some of these changes give rise  to problems that can make them a drag  while combat is solid when fighting a  limited number of enemies things quickly  turn south when more enemies are added  into the mix turning bigger battles into  clunky affairs this is especially  problematic with a walk on camera that  follows the action for two closely  turning battles that could be tactical  and fierce into disorienting and clumsy  encounters in some cases it felt better  fighting without lock-on enabled to  better keep track of what's going on  this clumsiness is even present in the  stealth system which is more awkward  than it was in past games somehow slower  and deliberate movement can feel  unreliable due to controls that feel  less accurate and unresponsive byuk can  sometimes hitch onto ledges and other  objects when he gets close enough  frequently resulting in accidental  exposure to enemies sneaking feels  undermined by these problems or there  comes from enemies seeing through  objects or instances where a our  partners routinely walk into danger  stealth is often more of a hassle than  it's worth making in one of the weaker  aspects of origins  Assassin's Creed origins attempts to  blend the established stealth action  elements with the mechanics of an action  RPG but these two halves don't always  coalesce by including staff building in  a Luke run it creates needless level  gating for areas of the map this results  in time spent grinding to acquire vital  skills and resources and in the broader  sense can feel like artificial padding  this in turn conflicts with the feeling  of being a skillful assassin who uses  resources to maneuver through enemy  hideouts on his wits and then finding  out your assassination won't work due to  an underpowered hidden blade assassin's  creed origins reaches great heights in  his new setting it routinely runs into  issues that bogged down the overall game  technical issues make for an uneven  experience and its new gameplay pillars  wobble under the weight of its systems  but despite this the world of origins  remains fresh and exciting to explore  which is a testament to the remarkable  setting and compelling story Assassin's  Creed is a series has undergone many  changes in its long and storied history  and origins feels like the first step in  the start of something new it has its  fair share of problems but the vision  for its future is one worth pursuing  [Applause]  [Music]  [Applause]  [Music]

